   NAME

	COPYMEMDMA - Gives the big memory copies of the system to the DMA.

   FORMAT

	COPYMEMDMA [CM] [CMQ] [THRESHOLD <bytes>] [REMOVE] [STATUS] [FORCE]

   TEMPLATE

	CM/S,CMQ/S,THRESHOLD/N,REMOVE/S,STATUS/S,FORCE/S

   PATH

	C:COPYMEMDMA

   FUNCTION

	COPYMEMDMA patches Exec's CopyMem() and CopyMemQuick() so that the large
	copies are done by the DMA engine of the Raspberry Pi, through
	brcm-dma.resource, instead of by the 68k. The task that copies sleeps
	while the DMA works: the 68k is free for the others.

	It is made for a PiStorm with Emu68 whose ROM holds brcm-dma.resource. It
	is first a stress test and a benchmark of the resource: every task of the
	system becomes a client of the DMA.

	CM        - Patch CopyMem().
	CMQ       - Patch CopyMemQuick().
	THRESHOLD - The smallest copy, in bytes, that goes to the DMA. Default
	            1048576. At least 1. A smaller copy is done by the routine that
	            was there before, at the price of one comparison. Under 16384 the
	            command refuses, unless FORCE is given.
	REMOVE    - Take the patch off.
	FORCE     - Accept a THRESHOLD under 16384 bytes. A DMA job costs about
	            150 microseconds whatever its size, the CPU copies a small block
	            in less than one: with a small threshold a program that copies
	            many small blocks (a MUI application, for instance) takes
	            minutes. For stress tests only.
	STATUS    - Show the state of the patch that is installed, without
	            changing it: the threshold, the copies done by the DMA, those
	            that were given back to the CPU after a job, those that the CPU
	            did because of the rules, and the tasks that are in the DMA
	            path right now, with the size of their copy. A task that stays
	            there while the system waits is the one that is stuck.

	At least one of CM and CMQ is needed to install the patch. There is no
	need to run the command in the background: it detaches itself, the shell
	gets its prompt back and the patch stays in memory.

	Started from Workbench, there are no arguments: both functions are
	patched, with the default threshold.

	A copy goes to the DMA only if all of this is true: its size reaches the
	THRESHOLD; the caller is a task that may sleep (no Forbid(), no
	Disable(), not an interrupt); the two blocks do not overlap; both lie in
	fast memory (not MEMF_CHIP, not MEMF_24BITDMA), outside the bus of the
	Amiga (below 2 MB: the Chip RAM; from 10 MB to 16 MB: PCMCIA, CIAs,
	chipset, clock, ROM) and outside the table
	of the 68k vectors (VBR, read when the patch is installed): those
	addresses are not in the RAM of the Pi, the DMA cannot reach them; enough
	stack is left for the calls into the resource. In every other case, and
	when the resource refuses a job or does not finish it, the copy is done
	by the routine that was there before: a copy is never lost, and the
	resource may be unavailable at any time.

	The addresses need no alignment: the DMA takes any address and any
	length. Both caches are looked after by the resource.

	If the command is started while the patch is installed, it says so and
	changes nothing. REMOVE waits for the copies that are in flight, puts the
	old vectors back, and prints how many copies went to the DMA and how
	many were given back to the CPU.

	The vectors are put back only if they still point to this patch. If
	another program patched the same function since, the vector cannot be
	taken out of the chain: the patch then stays in memory, passes every call
	on to the routine that was there before, and REMOVE can be tried again
	later.

   NOTES

	The patch is a process that sleeps, named "copymemdma", and a public
	semaphore of the same name that holds the state. Do not break the
	process by hand (CTRL_C from a shell is not sent to it): use REMOVE.

	The copies below the THRESHOLD are not counted. A low THRESHOLD (a few
	kilobytes) sends many small jobs to the resource and slows the system
	down: it is meant for testing. The copies of the 68k are faster than a
	DMA job up to a size that depends on the machine; measure it before
	choosing a value.

	The DMA is shared with the drivers that use the resource (the RTG
	driver, for instance). Watch it with BRCM-DMA-INFO WATCH.

	For further information, please see:
	https://github.com/flype44/brcm-dma.resource

   EXAMPLES

	> copymemdma CM CMQ
	  Patch both functions, from 1 MB.

	> copymemdma CMQ THRESHOLD 65536
	  Patch CopyMemQuick() only, from 64 KB.

	> copymemdma CM CMQ THRESHOLD=4096 FORCE
	  A low threshold, for a stress test.

	> copymemdma REMOVE
	  Take the patch off.

   RETURN CODES

	SUCCESS (0)  Installed, or removed.
	WARN    (5)  Already installed, not installed (REMOVE), or the vector
	             could not be put back (see FUNCTION).
	ERROR   (10) Bad arguments, neither CM nor CMQ given, brcm-dma.resource
	             not present or its engine not available, or the process
	             could not be created.
	FAIL    (20) dos.library 36 or newer is not available.

   SEE ALSO

	BRCM-DMA-INFO, BRCM-DMA-TEST, SETPATCH
